<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
        The FCM receiver, declared in the library that owns it rather than in :app's
        manifest, so that the module boundary above it means something: the service
        class, the Firebase dependency and the manifest entry that activates it are
        one unit and move together.

        `exported="false"` — the intent filter below is matched by an explicit
        broadcast from Google Play services within the app's own process boundary,
        and nothing else has any business starting it. `FirebaseMessagingService`'s
        own manifest entry in the AAR is a fallback that does nothing; this one wins
        by being more specific and is what routes messages to BittrMessagingService.

        No `android:permission` attribute: the intent is delivered by the Play
        services client library in-process, not by a remote sender.

        THE ONLY ONE (BIT-146). This entry is also the background wake's wiring
        (BIT-133), so losing it is silent twice over: the AAR's fallback entry
        sits at android:priority="-500" and has an empty onMessageReceived, so
        a missing entry neither crashes nor logs. FcmWakeTest asserts this
        service out-ranks the fallback on the installed package.

        No android:priority. The default is 0 and the fallback is -500, so this
        already wins; a number here would make the ordering look like tuning.

        NOT android:directBootAware. The base class sets it, and inheriting it
        would be wrong: the seed is behind a Keystore key whose material is not
        available before first unlock, so a wake in direct-boot mode has nothing
        to start against (BIT-8 rule 2). A child <service> does not inherit the
        library's attributes, so no tools:replace is needed.
    -->
    <application>
        <service
            android:name=".BittrMessagingService"
            android:exported="false">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="com.google.firebase.MESSAGING_EVENT" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>
    </application>

</manifest>
